# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CODEOWNERS - terraform-azure-aks-platform
#
# CODEOWNERS on its own is only a *suggestion*: it auto-requests a review from
# the listed owners and nothing more. The teeth come from pairing it with
# branch protection on `main`:
#
#     Settings -> Branches -> Branch protection rule for `main`
#       [x] Require a pull request before merging
#       [x] Require approvals (>= 1)
#       [x] Require review from Code Owners     <-- this is the enforcement
#       [x] Dismiss stale approvals when new commits are pushed
#       [x] Require status checks: terraform-plan / detect-changes, security
#       [x] Do not allow bypassing the above settings
#
# With "Require review from Code Owners" enabled, a PR that touches any path
# below cannot merge until the matching owner approves. That is what turns the
# sensitive-path rules further down from documentation into a control.
#
# Matching rules: last match wins, so ordering is significant - the catch-all
# comes first and progressively narrower, more sensitive paths follow.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Default owner for everything in the repository.
*                           @fakhfakhahmed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production environment.
# Changes here bill real money and affect the cluster that serves traffic.
# Nothing merges into envs/prod/ without an explicit owner approval, and the
# apply is additionally gated by the `prod` GitHub Environment's required
# reviewers (see .github/workflows/terraform-apply.yml).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/envs/prod/                 @fakhfakhahmed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Policy-as-code.
# These files ARE the guardrails. A PR that weakens an OPA rule or deletes an
# Azure Policy definition is functionally a request to lower the security bar,
# and must be reviewed as such rather than slipping through as "a .rego tweak".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/policy/                    @fakhfakhahmed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI/CD definitions.
# The workflows hold the OIDC trust configuration, the environment approval
# gates and the cost safety rails. Anyone who can edit a workflow can remove
# the `environment:` key that forces manual approval before `terraform apply`,
# or add a trigger to terraform-destroy.yml. Self-modifying CI is the classic
# supply-chain escalation path - owner review is mandatory here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.github/workflows/         @fakhfakhahmed
/.github/dependabot.yml     @fakhfakhahmed
/.github/CODEOWNERS         @fakhfakhahmed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bootstrap stack.
# Creates the remote state storage account, the Entra ID application and the
# federated credential that every other workflow authenticates with. Breaking
# this does not break one environment - it breaks the ability to manage any of
# them, and recovery is a manual, credentialled operation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/bootstrap/                 @fakhfakhahmed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security and linting configuration.
# Relaxing a Checkov skip-list or a tflint rule changes what CI is willing to
# let through. Reviewed alongside whatever change motivated the relaxation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.checkov.yml               @fakhfakhahmed
/.tflint.hcl                @fakhfakhahmed
